{"id":"waterfall_mist","compound_name":"Graphs::MPM::waterfall_mist","source_path":"waterfall_mist/waterfall_mist.json","category":"Smoke","author":"Autodesk","description":"An MPM fluid simulation sourcing aero to produce mist","documentation_file":"waterfall_mist_docs_ENU.md","documentation":"# Waterfall Mist Graph\n\n### What it does\n\nThis graph uses the MPM system to simulate fluid from which an aero simulation is sourced to produce mist\n\n### Inputs and Outputs\n\nThis graph uses several bifrost geometries acting as sources for the fluid, collision geo and kill bounds.  These compounds are made of basic primitives, which have been merged and to which noise has applied where applicable. Note that specific materials have been applied to some of these geometries using material reference nodes in the graph. Also, individual output nodes are used to display these geometries in the viewport, while keeping the graph clean.\n\n### Useful information\n\n- The graph is made up of two major sections, as outlined by the backdrops. The first one on the left is the MPM simulation for the fluid, and the second on the right is the aero simulation.\n\n- The fluid simulation is fairly straightforward, not requiring much modification from the default values on its constituent compounds.  Of special note is the source_mpm_fluid which has a high `inherit velocity` to utilize the mesh noise deformations from the input geometry to inject some random velocities to the mist at sourcing time, and break up the flow a little. An absolute kill field is coupled with a mask to create a kill box for the particles. \n\n- The output particles from the MPM simulation are used as source geo for the aero simulation.  Before they are passed into the source_air however, some vary_source_propery nodes are chained together to add random variations to the `inherit_velocity`, `fog_density` and `temperature` values each mpm particle will have at souring time for aero.  \n\n- An array of influence fields are used on the aero simulation to dissipate fog, add vorticity, drag, and turbulence (note this is shared with the mpm sim), with modulations related to `fog_density` or `temperature values`. Note the arrangement which adds temperature into the system (the `temperature_mode` on the source_air is set to add) at every frame, which eventually results in rising mist, which is in turn dissipated when the temperature passes a certain threshold, via the modulation.  \n\n- This type of simulation would work nicely with Bifrost Legacy Fluids too, exported as a cache and read back in via read_bif_particles, and then fed into the aero system.","library_dependencies":{"Amino":"2.15.0.9","bif":"2.15.0.9","fields":"2.15.0.9","fluids":"2.15.0.9","geometries":"2.15.0.9","mpm":"2.15.0.9","simulation":"2.15.0.9"},"graph_ports":[{"portName":"collision_geo","portDirection":"output","portType":"Object"},{"portName":"emission_geo","portDirection":"output","portType":"Object"},{"portName":"fluid_particles","portDirection":"output","portType":"array<Object>"},{"portName":"aero_mist","portDirection":"output","portType":"Object"}],"terminal_states":[{"name":"Core::Graph::terminal::final","enabled":"false"},{"name":"Core::Graph::terminal::proxy","enabled":"false"},{"name":"Core::Graph::terminal::diagnostic","enabled":"false"}],"counts":{"nodes":51,"connections":63,"values":140,"compounds":5},"node_types":{"Modeling::Primitive::create_mesh_cube":5,"Rendering::Materials::assign_material":3,"Simulation::Common::vary_source_property":3,"Core::Math::multiply":3,"Simulation::Common::collider":2,"Simulation::Influence::modulate_influence":2,"Rendering::Materials::standard_surface_mat":2,"Modeling::Common::merge_geometry":2,"Value::float":2,"Simulation::MPM::simulate_mpm":1,"Simulation::MPM::mpm_solver_settings":1,"Simulation::Influence::kill_influence":1,"Simulation::Influence::mask_influence":1,"Simulation::MPM::source_mpm_fluid":1,"noisy_spheres":1,"Simulation::Aero::aero_solver_settings":1,"S